## 4. Universal Clean Code Principles

Apply these principles regardless of programming language.

### 4.1 Meaningful Naming

All identifiers should communicate their purpose.

Evaluate:

- Variables
- Functions
- Methods
- Classes
- Modules
- Interfaces
- Types
- Constants
- Files
- Directories
- Database entities

Rules:

- Use descriptive names.
- Avoid ambiguous abbreviations.
- Avoid meaningless names.
- Boolean names should communicate conditions.
- Function names should communicate behavior.
- Class names should communicate responsibility.
- Maintain naming consistency.

Follow the naming conventions of the detected language.

### 4.2 Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

Every component should have one coherent responsibility.

Identify:

- Classes handling unrelated operations.
- Functions performing multiple unrelated tasks.
- Modules with excessive responsibilities.
- Components that mix business logic and infrastructure.
- Modules that are difficult to test independently.

Recommend separation only when it improves cohesion and maintainability.

### 4.3 Don't Repeat Yourself (DRY)

Identify unnecessary duplication in:

- Business logic
- Validation
- Data transformations
- Database operations
- Configuration
- Error handling
- Repeated conditional logic

Create abstractions only when they genuinely reduce maintenance costs.

Do not confuse duplicated syntax with duplicated business knowledge.

### 4.4 Keep It Simple (KISS)

Prefer the simplest correct implementation.

Avoid:

- Unnecessary abstraction.
- Excessive nesting.
- Overcomplicated expressions.
- Unnecessary inheritance.
- Excessive design patterns.
- Unnecessary dependencies.
- Clever but difficult-to-understand code.

### 4.5 You Aren't Gonna Need It (YAGNI)

Avoid implementing hypothetical requirements without evidence.

Identify:

- Unused features.
- Unnecessary extensibility.
- Dead code.
- Premature abstractions.
- Unused dependencies.
- Unnecessary configuration.
- Overengineered components.

### 4.6 Small and Focused Functions

Functions and methods should:

- Have a clear purpose.
- Minimize side effects.
- Avoid excessive parameters.
- Avoid excessive nesting.
- Have predictable behavior.
- Be easy to test.
- Use meaningful names.

Evaluate logical complexity rather than enforcing arbitrary line limits.

### 4.7 Separation of Concerns

Keep responsibilities separated across appropriate boundaries.

Evaluate separation between:

- Presentation
- Business Logic
- Data Access
- Infrastructure
- External Integrations
- Authentication
- Authorization
- Validation
- Configuration
- Error Handling

Apply architecture according to project requirements rather than forcing a predefined architecture.

### 4.8 Error Handling

Review:

- Exception handling.
- Error propagation.
- Error logging.
- Recovery strategies.
- Resource cleanup.
- Transaction safety.
- API error responses.
- Failure isolation.

Never silently ignore important errors.

Never expose internal stack traces or sensitive information to end users.

### 4.9 Dependency Management

Evaluate:

- Dependency injection.
- Dependency direction.
- Circular dependencies.
- Unnecessary dependencies.
- Version compatibility.
- Package maintenance.
- Dependency security.
- Coupling between modules.

Prefer explicit and manageable dependencies.

### 4.10 Comments and Documentation

Identify:

- Redundant comments.
- Outdated documentation.
- Misleading explanations.
- Missing documentation for complex behavior.
- Missing public API documentation.

Prefer self-documenting code.

Use comments to explain WHY rather than simply repeating WHAT the code does.

### 4.11 Type Safety

Evaluate the type system of the detected language.

Check:

- Appropriate type annotations.
- Incorrect type assumptions.
- Unsafe type conversions.
- Nullability.
- Optional values.
- Runtime type errors.
- Generic type usage.
- Type contracts.
- Unnecessary dynamic typing.

Use the strongest practical type guarantees supported by the language and project.

Do not introduce unnecessary type complexity.

### 4.12 Immutability and State Management

Evaluate:

- Unnecessary mutable state.
- Shared mutable objects.
- Unexpected side effects.
- State synchronization.
- Data ownership.
- Concurrency issues.
- Race conditions.

Prefer predictable state transitions and immutability where appropriate.

### 4.13 Testability

Review whether components can be tested independently.

Evaluate:

- Unit testability.
- Integration testability.
- Dependency isolation.
- External service boundaries.
- Side effects.
- Deterministic behavior.
- Test data management.
- Test coverage of critical business rules.

Recommend appropriate testing strategies for the detected ecosystem.

