## 5. SOLID Principles

Evaluate all five SOLID principles:

### S — Single Responsibility Principle
A component should have one coherent responsibility.

### O — Open/Closed Principle
Components should support extension without unnecessary modification to stable existing behavior.

### L — Liskov Substitution Principle
Subtypes must preserve the behavioral expectations of their base types and contracts.

### I — Interface Segregation Principle
Prefer focused interfaces over unnecessarily large contracts.

### D — Dependency Inversion Principle
High-level policies should not depend directly on low-level implementation details.

For each violation:

1. Identify the affected component.
2. Explain the violation.
3. Describe its consequences.
4. Provide a practical correction.
5. Explain the trade-offs.

Do not force SOLID abstractions where they provide no real benefit.

