## 13. Mandatory Review Report Format

# Universal Clean Code Review Report

## 1. Project Overview

- Detected programming languages.
- Detected frameworks.
- Runtime versions.
- Architecture.
- Relevant dependencies.

## 2. Executive Summary

Summarize the overall quality and most important findings.

## 3. Quality Score

Score each category from 1 to 10:

| Category | Score |
|---|---|
| Readability | /10 |
| Maintainability | /10 |
| Architecture | /10 |
| Security | /10 |
| Performance | /10 |
| Testability | /10 |
| Reliability | /10 |
| Standards Compliance | /10 |

Scores must be supported by observed evidence.

Mark unassessed categories as Not Assessed rather than inventing a score.

## 4. Critical & High Priority Findings

List the most important issues first.

For every finding:

- Title
- Severity
- Location
- Problem
- Impact
- Recommended Fix

## 5. Clean Code Analysis

Evaluate:

- Naming
- SRP
- DRY
- KISS
- YAGNI
- Function Complexity
- Separation of Concerns
- Error Handling
- Dependency Management
- Type Safety
- Testability

## 6. SOLID Analysis

Evaluate all five SOLID principles individually.

## 7. Language-Specific Standards

Report compliance with relevant standards and conventions.

## 8. Security Analysis

Report confirmed vulnerabilities and potential risks separately.

## 9. Performance Analysis

Report evidence-based performance concerns.

## 10. Architecture Analysis

Evaluate architectural strengths, weaknesses, coupling, and cohesion.

## 11. Refactoring Recommendations

Provide actionable recommendations ordered by priority.

## 12. Improved Code Examples

Show original and improved implementations.

## 13. Validation Results

Report:

- Tests executed.
- Tests passed.
- Tests failed.
- Linters executed.
- Static analysis results.
- Unverified changes.

## 14. Final Assessment

Summarize:

- Existing strengths.
- Immediate fixes.
- Recommended improvements.
- Unnecessary changes to avoid.
- Suggested next steps.

